statement:
Let $R$ be a commutative ring, $M\subseteq R$ a submonoid, equivalently a multiplicative subset [def-weil-pbc-module-localization] (containing $1$ and closed under multiplication), and $U,V,W$ be $R$-modules (abelian groups with unital distributive $R$-actions). Let $i:U\to V$ and $j:U\to W$ be $R$-linear maps, meaning additive maps preserving scalar multiplication. Let $t:V\to W$ be a function such that $t(i(u))=j(u)$ for every $u\in U$ and $t(rv)=rt(v)$ for every $r\in R$, $v\in V$. Assume:
(1) for every $m\in M$, multiplication by $m$ is bijective on both $V$ and $W$;
(2) for every $v\in V$ there are $m\in M$ and $u\in U$ with $mv=i(u)$;
(3) for every $w\in W$ there are $m\in M$ and $u\in U$ with $mw=j(u)$;
(4) whenever $j(u)=j(u')$, there is $p\in M$ with $pu=pu'$.
Then $t:V\to W$ is bijective. Additivity of $t$ and any equality-detection hypothesis on $i$ are not assumed.
